If an error occurs (for example, an incorrect READ file name), the prompt is   
replaced with an appropriate warning. Pressing RETURN witrolled using a number of menus which require only   
a selector number followed by RETURN to call a function. When VIEWTEXT is run  
the MAIN MENU is displayed. This menu selects the most commonly used functions.
The COMMUNICATIONS menu and CONFIGURATIll restore the prompt. 
All VIEWTEXT menus behave in this manner, although certain functions do not    
require a RETURN key (see Directories). As a general rule, functions which do  
not require a RETURN cannot do any damage or change or erase files.  ON menu are called from the MAIN menu   
(functions 6 and 7).                                                           
                                                                               
Each menu displays the current date in the top left           
                                                                               
To return to the MAIN MENU from most functions the ESCAPE key is pressed twice.
The directories also use the dual ESCAPE mechanism to exit from sub-functions, 
shand corner or the string  
"NO DATE ENTERED" if the current date has not been set. The menus are all      
similar in appearance but are identified on the top line of the menu. All of   
the cursor control keys except BACKSPACE are inactive when a menuo the ESCAPE key must be pressed twice again to return to MAIN MENU. Note that
ESCAPE followed by a character other than ESCAPE is a valid key code on some   
computer systems (cursor moves etc), and also ESCAPE'd characters can be sent  
to the modem,  is displayed.
                                                                               
